.TH FSTABCTL 1 "2026" "fstabctl" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fstabctl \- manage, back up, restore and export fstab and home\-folder symlinks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fstabctl
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fstabctl
lists, adds, edits, backs up, restores, verifies and exports /etc/fstab entries, and manages
symlinks from the home folder to data disks. Every change is validated, shown as a diff and
backed up automatically before it is written. Commands that change /etc/fstab need root.
.PP
Options accepted by all commands that change something:
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show the diff or plan; write nothing.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Do not ask for confirmation.
.TP
.BI \-\-fstab " PATH"
Operate on this file instead of /etc/fstab.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list
Show fstab entries with live status (mounted, not mounted, device missing, disabled).
.TP
.B scan
Show disks and partitions and which are not in fstab.
.TP
.BR add " [\fIDEVICE\fR [\fIMOUNTPOINT\fR]]"
Add an entry. With no device, an interactive wizard runs. Options:
.BR \-t ", " \-\-fstype ;
.BR \-o ", " \-\-options " (overrides the preset);"
.BR \-p ", " \-\-preset " {data,ssd,usb,windows,nfs,cifs};"
.BR \-\-by " {uuid,label,partuuid,path};"
.BR \-\-dump ;
.BR \-\-passno ;
.BR \-\-no\-mkdir .
.TP
.BI set " TARGET"
Change an entry:
.BR \-o ", " \-\-add\-options ", " \-\-remove\-options ", " \-t ", " \-\-device ", " \-\-mountpoint ", " \-\-passno .
.TP
.BI remove " TARGET"
Delete an entry. Boot\-critical mounts (/, /boot, /boot/efi, /efi) need
.BR \-\-force .
.TP
.BI disable " TARGET"
Comment an entry out, keeping it for later (marker #fstabctl\-disabled#).
.TP
.BI enable " TARGET"
Re\-enable a disabled entry.
.PP
.BR set ", " remove ", " disable ", " enable " and " mount
also accept
.BI \-\-expect " SPEC"\fR:
act only if the entry's device is still SPEC. This guards a
.I TARGET
given as a # number against an fstab that changed in the meantime.
.TP
.RI mount " [TARGET]"
Mount an entry now, without rebooting.
.B \-\-all
mounts every enabled entry that should be mounted but isn't, skipping disks that are not connected, entries
that mount on first access (noauto, x\-systemd.automount) and boot\-critical mounts. Runs
.B systemctl daemon\-reload
first on systemd systems. Needs root.
.TP
.B repair
Fix a line that can't be read.
.BI \-\-comment\-out " LINE"
puts a # in front of it;
.BI \-\-add\-defaults " LINE"
completes a line that has only device, mount point and type with "defaults" (plus nofail for
non\-system mounts) and "0 0". Refuses lines that are not broken.
.BI \-\-expect " TEXT"
acts only if the line still reads TEXT.
.TP
.B backup
Save a timestamped copy of fstab (\fB\-\-note\fR TEXT).
.TP
.B backups
List saved backups.
.TP
.RI diff " [BACKUP]"
Compare the current fstab with a backup (default: latest).
.TP
.RI restore " [BACKUP]"
Restore from a backup ID, unique prefix, "latest" or file path. The current file is backed up first.
.TP
.B verify
Check for errors and risky settings; exit status 1 if errors are found.
.TP
.B report
Human\-readable report.
.BR \-f " text|markdown, " \-o " FILE."
.TP
.B export
Write a portable JSON bundle (entries, disk inventory, home links).
.BR \-o " FILE."
.TP
.BI import " BUNDLE"
Merge a bundle into this system's fstab.
.BR \-\-include\-system ", " \-\-skip\-missing .
.TP
.BI link " ACTION"
Manage symlinks in the home folder: list, check, add, set, remove, backup, backups, restore, import,
discover (search home for links), track and untrack (keep links in backups and bundles), and folders
(Key folders: Documents, Downloads, Music, Pictures, Videos and your own). See
.B fstabctl link ACTION \-\-help
for each.
Options:
.BR \-\-home ", " \-\-depth ", " \-\-hidden ;
for add:
.BR \-\-adopt ", " \-\-force ", " \-\-relative ", " \-\-mkdir\-target ", " \-\-allow\-missing .
.TP
.B gui
Open the desktop app, fstab Manager,
.BR fstab\-manager (1).
.TP
.B presets
List the option presets used by
.BR add .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B FSTABCTL_FSTAB
fstab file to operate on (default /etc/fstab).
.TP
.B FSTABCTL_BACKUP_DIR
Directory for backups and snapshots.
.TP
.B FSTABCTL_REAL_USER
User whose home and ownership are used when running as root (SUDO_USER is the fallback).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/fstab
The file being managed.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/fstabctl/backups/
Backups (fstab\-ID.bak and .json) and link snapshots (links\-ID.json).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, nothing to change, or a dry run.
.TP
.B 1
Error; or verify found errors; or link check found broken links.
.TP
.B 2
A change was refused because it would introduce a validation problem, or a usage error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fstab\-manager (1),
.BR fstab (5),
.BR findmnt (8),
.BR mount (8)
.PP
Full documentation: /usr/share/doc/fstabctl/
